@page "/tooling"

@* What the package carries for the app's own files rather than its pages: the class check in
   its build, the rename script, the Stylelint config and the agent skill. Each snippet is a
   file under Examples/Tooling/. *@

<CatHead Title="App tooling" Tier="@CatHead.Tooling">
    The package checks the app's own files as well as styling its pages: its build warns on a class a
    release renamed or removed, a script applies the renames, a Stylelint config keeps the app's CSS off
    the library's classes, and a skill hands an agent the rules.
</CatHead>

<div class="callout callout--info">
    <i class="ri-archive-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">Matched to the version the app installed</span>
        Everything here ships inside the <code>Sedna.UI</code> package, under
        <code>~/.nuget/packages/sedna.ui/&lt;version&gt;/</code> once it is restored. Nothing is a
        second package or a package reference: the class check is MSBuild, and the rest are files.
    </div>
</div>

<h2 id="class-check">The class check in the build</h2>
<p class="cat-lede">
    On by default. After each build it reads the app's <code>.razor</code>, <code>.cshtml</code>,
    <code>.html</code> and <code>.css</code> files and warns once per use of a class that no longer
    exists, with the file and the line, so an IDE jumps to it.
</p>

<CatSnippet Name="Tooling/ClassCheckOutput.txt" Title="What the build says">
    <p>
        <code>SEDNAUI001</code> is a class a release renamed, with the class or classes that look the
        same now; <code>SEDNAUI002</code> is a class a release removed with nothing alike. In markup a
        class counts as a whole token, in a stylesheet as a selector, so <code>my-btn-go</code> and
        <code>btn-go-x</code> are never reported. They stay warnings under
        <code>TreatWarningsAsErrors</code>.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatSnippet Name="Tooling/ClassCheckOptions.txt" Title="Turning it off or narrowing it">
    <p>
        The renames come from each release's map in the package and the removals from its class
        history, so the check never names a class the installed version has.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<h2 id="renames">Applying the renames</h2>
<p class="cat-lede">
    <code>tooling/rename-classes.py</code> rewrites the app's files by every rename between two versions.
    It needs Python 3 and nothing else.
</p>

<CatSnippet Name="Tooling/RenameDryRun.txt" Title="Dry run, then write">
    <p>
        <code>--from</code> is the version the app had; <code>--to</code> defaults to the newest release in the package.
        A class is replaced whole, never as a prefix, and one that became two is written as two
        &mdash; two tokens in markup, one compound selector in a stylesheet. A class already on the
        element is not written twice. A removed class is left alone: the release notes say what replaces it.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<h2 id="stylelint">Stylelint</h2>
<p class="cat-lede">
    A config generated from the stylesheet of the same version. It reports a selector that names a
    class the library owns and a colour literal anywhere but as a token's value in
    <code>brand.css</code>, and holds <code>brand.css</code> to the tokens the library declares.
</p>

<CatSnippet Name="Tooling/StylelintConfig.txt" Title="Extend it">
    <p>
        Copy the file beside the app's own Stylelint config and extend it from there: its
        <code>brand.css</code> rules are matched relative to the file. Copy it again after each upgrade,
        so the class list is the installed version's.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatSnippet Name="Tooling/StylelintOutput.txt" Title="What it reports">
    <p>
        A rule for a library class is the override the <a href="/concepts">concepts</a> page rules out:
        set a token, use a variant, or put a class with the app's prefix on the element.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<h2 id="skill">The agent skill</h2>
<p class="cat-lede">
    The rules an agent follows in a consuming app, as a skill: the same text as the
    <code>CLAUDE.md</code> block in the repository's <code>docs/CLAUDE.consuming-app.md</code>, with
    front matter.
</p>

<CatSnippet Name="Tooling/SkillInstall.txt" Title="Install it">
    <p>
        Copy the folder into the app's <code>.claude/skills/</code>. Use the skill or the
        <code>CLAUDE.md</code> block, not both. Pair it with the <a href="/mcp-server">MCP server</a>,
        which the skill tells the agent to use for every piece of markup.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>
